## Building Your Personal Standard Library

This capstone brings together three modules:

### 1. my_math Module

```xiom
mod my_math {
  pub fn factorial(n: Int) -> Int { ... }
  pub fn fibonacci(n: Int) -> Int { ... }
  pub fn is_prime(n: Int) -> Bool { ... }
  pub fn gcd(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { ... }
  pub fn lcm(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { ... }
}
```

### 2. my_string Module

```xiom
mod my_string {
  pub fn reverse(s: Str) -> Str { ... }
  pub fn count_words(s: Str) -> Int { ... }
  pub fn capitalize(s: Str) -> Str { ... }
  pub fn truncate(s: Str, max: Int) -> Str { ... }
  pub fn is_palindrome(s: Str) -> Bool { ... }
}
```

### 3. my_collections Module

```xiom
interface Stack[T] {
  fn push(&mut self, item: T);
  fn pop(&mut self) -> Option[T];
  fn peek(&self) -> Option[&T];
}

type VecStack[T] {
  items: Vec[T];
}
```

### Integration

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  use my_math;
  use my_string;
  use my_collections;

  let fib = my_math.fibonacci(10);
  let rev = my_string.reverse("hello");
  io.println(fib);
  io.println(rev);
  return 0;
}
```

You now have reusable tools. Every future program can start with `use my_math;`.

